Z||000006||mshill||10-08-2001||12:08 PM||||Slammed, what size springs (diameter, length, rate) are you running.  I see on the stockcarproducts page there is a 5 inch and a 7 inch collar, which one is needed for the Fiero setup?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||10-08-2001||02:36 PM||||The problem is adjustability.<P>I installed the Ground control kit as is and found that the treaded sleeve is much to short. I had it set as low as it would go. Mind you have to define the cars purpose. I run a 325lbs rear spring and koni's set full hard. Needless to say the car is not a daily driver.<P>In order to get the sleeve to fit properly on the koni , I had to notch the sleeve itself. The collar ont he strut body where your factory spring would sit , is not a flat surface. It has been stamped to accept a stock type spring. Therefore the sleeve would not sit level on the strut collar. This is not a good ideaa. Ground controls collars are made of aluminum , one has to wonder about the implications of stress on the threaded sleeve , espically since it wasn't sitting flat on the collar.<P>I ground mine to sit perfectly on the strut.(2 notches)<BR>None of this really needs to apply if you just wanta qucik way to puton coilovers. GC is a great setup , but I wanted more adjustability in the setup.<P>Now as for stock car coilover parts. This was basically to illustrate how cheap these setups are. The GC kit comes with eibachs. These are nice springs , But after a while (a couple years) they tend to sag. I don't know if the same can be said for other manufacturers , but eibachs are  known to sag after a while. This doesn't mean there bad springs , this is just from what I have heard from PCA members and on the Fiero Racing list.<P>As for springs. Don't go too high on the rate. My car rides terrbile , even set on soft , it rides like a covered wagon , but in order to make a fiero handle (84 to 87) you have to give up a few things.  I just ran out to the garage and measured the Dia of a Koni strut.  The strut body has a dia of 2" so ultimatley you want a threaded sleeve that fits snugly over the body of the strut.<P>To make a long story short , I had a new threaded sleeve made , and I'm using the Eibachs that came with the ground control kit.<P>Want a better setup the GC? Here's what I'd do if I had to do it again.<P>First Get the Carrera 7" (height) coilover sleeve from Stock car products (the link I posted)<P>Remove the strut from your car. Very carefully remove the collar that the original spring sat on. This is a lot easier than you think. The strut is held in by the bolts on the top of the shock tower , and the 2 on the lower strut body (the big Mothers) There is no need for a spring compressor , once you jack up the car so that the weight is off the wheels you will be fine.<P>Use a open shoe grinder to take the collar off. NO torches! compressed gas + torch = bomb.<P>Now this is the area that you need to pay attention in. You need the threaded sleeve to sit on something. This means the replacement of the collar at a lower height than original. I took my strut and put it in the freezer for 1 hr. I'm scared of blowing it up. This as you may know will reduce the psi of the gas in the strut , the colder it gets the slower molicules move ..ect..ect<P>I tack welded the new collar in place..( just use a cut down version of the old collar) Then I proceded to fill in the tack welds with more and more , beign careful not ot hod the mig welder on the strut for extended periods of time.<P>Slide over the threaded sleeve , Put on  your spring , reinstall strut with a lot more adjustability than the GC setup.<P>Sounds hard , but it's a lot easier than I make it sound. You could do both sides in about 4 hours. (12 if your like me) <P>As for spring rates. i would go 300lbs max!. It's cool to setup a car to corner like a slot car, BUT trust me when I say this , it will take the joy out of a daily driver pretty fast.<P>After you get that done I can tell us how to install the massive swaybar from a AWD Astro van , and you can scrap those weiner aftermarket sissy bars. Once again ride quality is not a concern.<P>the pic I have attached is my car set just about full low , with 17" wheels. I wouldn't attempt ot drive it on the street in this fashion, I did once and had 2 rear swaybar bushings ripped right off because of a pothole. This works great on a track or autocross though. Anyone who knows fiero handling , nows that one of the tricks is to get the car low as possible while retaining suspension travel. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar_1.JPG"> <P>Happy cornering!<P>JM<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||mshill||10-08-2001||03:19 PM||||So it sounds like with the GC you couldn't go low enough because the perch was too high, right?  The longer collar gave and the lowering of the perch gave you a better range to work with, correct?<P>||||reg||1
